<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.7.6: Lesson Synthesis</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00960</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.7.6: Lesson Synthesis</md:title>
    <md:uuid>7a6a05a4-f76a-4d41-91cf-e6fb9f9070f3</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Some key takeaways from this lesson are that equations can be written in different forms. Often, one form is better than another based on the information given.</para>
<list>
  <item> Ask students to create their own problems and write the equation three different ways. </item>
  <item> Show that an equation in all three forms is equivalent by substituting a point into all three equations. </item>
  <item> Continue to have students use formal mathematical vocabulary around slope and the different forms. </item>
</list>
<para>In the next lesson, students will see how slope as rate of change occurs in real-world situations.</para>
</section>
</content>
